A||||4||loafer87gt||Need some advice ||||1||
Z||000000||loafer87gt||04-19-2001||01:56 PM||||I'm really at a loss as to what to do with my Fiero. It has now been sitting for two years with a bad engine making it undriveable. I parked the car when the engine started to make a clacking noise, and I have no idea what the problem is. One GM dealership told me it was a bad lifter, another told me it was rod knock, and my home mechanic thinks its a bad bearing. The noise does not start until the car has ran for a couple of minutes until it warms up, and then it starts. My oil pressure is fine, but something defintely wrong with the engine. As much as it saddens me, I am thinking I might be forced to sell it with the blown engine to help pay off my loan. What do you guys think would be the best route to go: Sell the car as is, not running or buy an engine from a wreckers and install it. (500 for the engine, 1000 for install). Either way, it's kinda depressing as I met with the bank on Monday and found out that so far in the past four years I have put the same amount in repairs into my car as I paid for the car when I bought it. I thought that I had hit the jackpot this spring as my buddy needed an engine to rebuild for his auto class, but unfortunately they already had lined up another motor for him to repair. I have priced out getting the engine rebuilt, and the absolute cheapest I have been able to find still says it will be at least 3000+ taxes to do a rebuild and install the motor. I also have to figure in a new EGR system and throttle linkage, adding another couple of hundred. SIGH. I got to stop throwing money at the car and hoping that someday it will run, and I am wondering how much I could get for the car in the present state with the motor the way it is. The car is an 87 GT, with 150,000 km, 5 speed, loaded, K & N, ported manifolds, Taylor plugwires, short shifter, Moog adjustable ball joints, new brake pads, new distributor, ECM, coil, cap, fully dynomatted interior, Viper car alarm with perimeter and radar modules, Sony Explode CD player with two Phoneix gold subs in custom box behind passenger seat, new paint, interior is in excellent shape except for a small (1/4 inch long) crack on center console. I also have two sets of tires, one winter and one summer, both with full tread. I'm at a loss as to what to do, I loved the car to death and babied like you wouldn't believe, but I have to draw the line somewhere on the spending. What would you guys do? Another fieroless summer for me I guess  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||btoth||04-19-2001||06:26 PM||||My lifters starting knocking bad three weeks ago... can't drive it.  I've spent the last three weeks debating wether to sell it as is, part it out (depressing thought), or buy a new engine.  I bought my GT for $3000 and put about $3000 into it and then this happens (I still have the Flowmaster muffler sitting on the living room floor... never got it installed).  I have no money because of school, and my parents are in need of their own car so they can't really afford to help me out (seeing as how I don't really <I>need</I> the car - I could always walk the 1.5 mi to my college).<P>Today, my parents decided to help me out and pay for half the new engine for my 20th birthday, which is coming up this weekend.  So I'm getting a remanufactured 2.8 for $1900 and installed for $800.  <A HREF="http://www.jasperengines.com" TARGET=_blank>www.jasperengines.com</A>  is a place I was told about by the guy at the local shop (they might have install places near you).  It even comes with a 3 year/75000 mi. warranty.<P>It seems that you really enjoy your car like I do and it would be depressing to let it go.  (Funny how these cars grow on you.)  If you can get a remanufactured engine like I am it would probably be your cheapest and best alternative to selling the car you love, and you'd probably be happier in the end.||||reg||7
Z||000002||rogergarrison||04-19-2001||06:34 PM||||You can buy a Jasper total rebuild long block for $1395 with an unlimited miles warranty. Assuming its a 2.8 V6. thats from my latest book listing.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 04-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||04-20-2001||07:31 AM||||Seems to me to replace or rebuild a motor on a hunch isn't the smartest move. I would find out if it's actually a lifter or a bearing. By your description, it's hard to know. But the truth is, unless the crank is really hosed, you can repair both of those problems for a small portion of the amount you're talking about spending for a rebuilt motor.<BR>If it helps identify the problem any, if you drive the car, if the noise seems to subside once in a while, such as when you're caosting, with no real load on the motor, it's likely a bearing. If it's always there, regardless of load, or throttle position, it's probably a lifter. I would without question be positive of the problem before investing a buttload of money into it that it may not need.<BR>Good luck<BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000004||Old Lar||04-20-2001||09:11 AM||||If you really liked the car, and the engine is toast, spending $3000-4000 on it to replace the bad engine is worth it when you compare the cost to that of another car with questionable history.  I replace a 5 cylinder 2.8 in my 1988 with a remanufactured 3.1, which had some problem which I did not want to frustrate myself locating.  The car now has a new 3.4L engine and new transmission.  It has been rebuilt to the way I wanted it. I was fortunate to have money put away that I could play with.  The big question is: can afford the costs associated with all the work that may be required?||||reg||10
